From 4788e55f1483cabdd3734a459c2058256b2979d0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel FORTE <2580288+g4bwy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 14:43:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 2/4] [WIP][Qwen4Exp] Read the QSA sparse attention from an
 int8 per-token-head cache

Completes the parked int8 KV lane, which could write the cache but refused to read
it. The kernel gains a PER_TOKEN_SCALE constexpr plus two fp32 scale pointers, and the
descales go where each is cheapest: K's rides on the score after the QK dot, one
multiply per token, and V's is applied to the value tile, which costs per element
because probabilities are already about 1/TOPK and scaling them pushes the fp16 dot
operand toward zero.

int8 needs a dynamic scale where e4m3 does not, e4m3 has an exponent and int8 does not,
and this checkpoint ships no KV scale tensors at all, its per-tensor host scales are the
default 1.0. So the scale is computed per (token, head) at write time and read back as a
tensor, which the padded cell that the previous commit introduced already carries.

Also fixes a latent bug in the write side of that commit, the data views it handed to the
reader were the padded half cell, 260 elements for a 256-wide head, so the launcher's
shape check would have rejected the very first forward. The views are narrowed to the head
width now. The warmup is made int8-aware with the same padded-cell arithmetic, otherwise
the specialization is first compiled at capture time.

Verified numerically against a float64 reference built straight from the padded cell, both
dispatch paths, 0.0017 and 0.0062 maximum absolute error against a 2.54 reference, and
regression-clean on the shipped arms, fp8 and bf16 still measure the same 0.994 time ratio
on this tree as on the shipped one after a signature that touched every call site.

Not for promotion. Both arms here still run the unfused prepare, so this isolates the read
and write arithmetic from the fusion loss, which is the next variable.
