From b57a32fd3beefed31f0163b93aeeb8a0f85320e8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel FORTE <2580288+g4bwy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 15:07:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] [WIP][Qwen4Exp] Fill the INT8 per-token-head cache from
 the fused QSA prepare

The fused prepare kernel was refused for a per-token-head cache because it
could only write one host scale per side. It now quantizes each (token, KV
head, side) itself and lands the fp32 scale in the cell's inline padding, so
one launch covers the indexer prepare, the main QK-norm/RoPE/gate, and the
int8 K/V write. The math mirrors the existing triton writer exactly: scale
absmax/127 with a 1e-6 floor, round halves away from zero before the int8
store truncates, clamp to [-128, 127]. The K absmax sees only the bytes the
row stores, so the values dropped by the rotation cannot set the scale.

The write side needs the scale views before the read side asks for them, so
the reader's _ensure_scale_caches is exposed as impl.pth_scale_caches; one
allocation serves both. Off the int8 lane the new pointer operands are
compiled out by the IS_INT8_PTH constexpr and a rank-matched stand-in rides
along.

Verified: cells and scales are byte-identical to the unfused writer across
11 shapes, and the bf16 and fp8 outputs are byte-identical to the pre-change
kernel, with mutation controls confirming the checks can fail. Kernel parity
is not model parity: the fused path quantizes its own fp32 norm/RoPE result
rounded through bf16, the unfused path reads flashinfer and triton rope
output, and the two can differ by about one bf16 ulp. Compare greedy tokens
fused against unfused before trusting int8 accuracy.
