From cd2d7eb305adfe55d5d2d9ff1cf78dc5f83652ad Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gabriel FORTE <2580288+g4bwy@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 21:55:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] [WIP][Qwen4Exp] Widen the per-token-head scale slot to 16
 bytes

The int8 lane ran 131k-prefill reads at 81 ms per call against bf16's 19 ms
on the identical grid, a whole-step trace of the live config showed. The
cause is the cell: K(256) + one 4-byte fp32 scale pad gives a head stride of
520, which Triton cannot prove divisible by 16, so every K and V column load
loses its vectorization. bf16 and fp8 cells are 512-byte aligned and never
hit this; decode hides it because one query row is microseconds either way.

The scale keeps riding in the cell padding, now in a 16-byte slot (fp32 at
the slot's front, 12 bytes of never-read pad behind it): half-width 272, cell
544, so every stride the kernels see is 16-byte aligned again. Layout math
moves from four literals to the single PTH_SCALE_SLOT_BYTES constant
(customize_spec, _ensure_scale_caches, the fused writer's V offset, the
prepare head-width inverse, the kernel warmup). The fused writer takes it as
a MAIN_PTH_SLOT constexpr.

Measured on the prefill grid (6464, 2, 1): read int8/bf16 4.92x at cell 520,
1.07x at cell 544. Fused-vs-unfused byte parity re-verified on the new
layout, all 11 checks, bf16/uint8 lanes still byte-identical. The QSA pool
grows 4.6% per token per head, which the boot pool will barely move: the
pool caps on a dtype-independent group. Test stub gained the kv_quant_mode
attribute it has been missing since the lane's first commit.
